---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import '../../styles/archive.css';

// PERMANENT ARCHIVE PAGE. Styling comes from src/styles/archive.css, which is
// theme-INDEPENDENT on purpose. Do not re-theme this page during a rebuild.
//
// It exists to hold a roske.ai copy of the only known recording of the 2007
// performance. The original lives on a YouTube channel Edward no longer
// controls, so the mp4 in public/video/ is the point of the whole page.

const YOUTUBE = 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A02xuVz_46c';
const FILE = '/video/eddie-and-the-consultants-2007.mp4';

// The story post ships on its own schedule, so only offer the link once the
// post actually exists in the collection. Otherwise this button would 404.
const STORY = 'i-produced-a-musical-about-financial-software';
const storyLive = (await getCollection('blog')).some((p) => p.id === STORY && !p.data.draft);

const cast = [
  { role: 'Eddie from IT', who: 'Edward Roske', note: 'Orlando, 2007' },
  { role: 'Eddie from IT', who: 'Tom Myers', note: 'Las Vegas, 2006' },
  { role: 'Penny from Accounting', who: 'Danielle White', note: 'Orlando, 2007. Pregnant, and right to be nervous about the lift' },
  { role: 'Penny from Accounting', who: 'Name lost', note: 'Las Vegas, 2006' },
  { role: 'Aquaman', who: 'John Vorhies, Jr.', note: 'On video in 2006, flown in and live in 2007' },
  { role: 'Director', who: 'A graduate of an actual film school', note: 'Name also lost' },
  { role: 'Book, music, and lyrics', who: 'Edward Roske', note: 'Written in 3 days, sick in bed' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Eddie and the Consultants: A System 9 Musical | Roske.AI"
  description="The full 58-minute recording of Eddie and the Consultants: A System 9 Musical, performed by interRel at the final Hyperion Solutions Conference in Orlando in 2007. Written, produced by, and starring Edward Roske."
  ogImage="/images/eddie-consultants/poster.jpg"
>
  <link slot="head" rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link slot="head" rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
  <link slot="head" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:ital,wght@0,500;0,700;0,900;1,500&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />

  <div class="arc">

    <section class="arc-top">
      <div class="arc-wrap">
        <div class="arc-eyebrow">
          <span class="arc-pip"></span>ARCHIVE . ORLANDO . 2007
        </div>
        <h1 class="arc-h1">Eddie and the Consultants</h1>
        <p class="arc-sub">A System 9 Musical</p>
        <div class="arc-rule"></div>
        <p class="arc-lede">
          58 minutes of musical theater about budgeting software, staged at the last Hyperion Solutions
          Conference before Oracle bought Hyperion and ended it. It started as a prank abstract written
          entirely in 50s and 60s rock parodies (nobody submits an abstract like that expecting a yes,
          which should tell you how much thought went into it), and then the reviewers accepted it, which
          meant somebody had to go find a cast, write the songs, talk an actual film school graduate into
          directing the thing, and make it exist in front of a room full of people who had flown in for a
          software conference.
        </p>

        <figure class="arc-player">
          <video
            controls
            preload="metadata"
            playsinline
            poster="/images/eddie-consultants/poster.jpg"
            src={FILE}
            width="1280"
            height="960"
          ></video>
          <figcaption>
            Recorded by interRel in Orlando, 2007. The camera was at the back of the room and the
            lighting was doing its best.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="arc-actions">
          <a class="arc-btn" href={FILE} download>Download the file (mp4)</a>
          <a class="arc-btn arc-btn-ghost" href={YOUTUBE} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Watch the original on YouTube</a>
          {storyLive && (
            <a class="arc-btn arc-btn-ghost" href={`/blog/${STORY}/`}>Read how this happened</a>
          )}
          <a class="arc-btn arc-btn-ghost" href="/video/">The rest of the archive</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="arc-sec">
      <div class="arc-wrap arc-wrap-narrow">
        <h2 class="arc-h2">The company</h2>
        <div class="arc-cast">
          {cast.map((c) => (
            <div class="arc-cast-row">
              <div class="arc-cast-role">{c.role}</div>
              <div class="arc-cast-who">
                <span class="arc-cast-name">{c.who}</span>
                <span class="arc-cast-note">{c.note}</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="arc-fine">
          Two of the names are gone and I would like them back. If you were in that room, or in that
          cast, Edward@Roske.AI.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="arc-sec arc-sec-alt">
      <div class="arc-wrap arc-wrap-narrow">
        <h2 class="arc-h2">Why this page exists</h2>
        <p class="arc-body">
          The musical ran twice: Las Vegas in 2006, where they broke down the airwall to the next room
          and it still wasn't big enough, and Orlando in 2007. interRel recorded it, pressed CDs and
          DVDs, mailed them to anyone who wrote in and asked, and for a while gave the entire news
          section of the company home page over to the cover art and an email address. Every one of
          those discs is gone now, at least the ones I had.
        </p>
        <p class="arc-body">
          In 2020 somebody at interRel (I still don't know who) put the recording on the company YouTube
          channel, where it sat at 45 views until I went looking for it in 2026. I don't control that
          channel anymore, so I put a copy here, on a site I actually pay for.
        </p>
        <p class="arc-body">
          Songs include CFO, which is Duke of Earl with new words, and the Summer of System 9, which is
          exactly the Bryan Adams song you are already hearing in your head. There is an Aquaman in it.
          (Edward, you wrote a jukebox musical about enterprise software while sick in bed, put a
          superhero in it, and then got on stage in Orlando and performed it for your own customers.
          Nobody asked you to do any of this.) I wrote the thing and I still can't tell you why.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="arc-sec">
      <div class="arc-wrap arc-wrap-narrow">
        <h2 class="arc-h2">The catch</h2>
        <p class="arc-body">
          The recording doesn't explain this part. Danielle was pregnant with her second child when we
          did Orlando, and the show ends with Penny leaping into Eddie's arms, and Eddie is me. She was
          genuinely worried I'd drop her, and by extension her daughter, and that was a completely fair
          thing to worry about. (Edward, you wrote the part, you cast yourself in the part, you flew
          everybody in for the rehearsals, and the one physical requirement in the entire show was
          catching a pregnant woman. At no point did it occur to you to write that out.) I caught her.
        </p>
        <p class="arc-body">
          The other thing you can't unsee once you've seen it is that Penny spends the whole show
          looking for a guy while Danielle was visibly pregnant, and we went ahead and staged it exactly
          like that, in front of a full room, in Orlando.
        </p>
        <p class="arc-body">
          Danielle is still in the EPM world. 18 years in Oracle EPM, now at Donyati, and elected
          Secretary of the ODTUG Executive Committee for FY26, which as far as I know makes her the only
          officer on that committee ever thrown at me in front of a full ballroom. I interviewed her at
          Kscope25 and we were both perfectly professional about it.
        </p>
        <p class="arc-body">
          If you were in either of those rooms, I'd love to hear what you remember, and if you know who
          directed it, even better. Edward@Roske.AI. Somebody out there knows!
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>
